---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../components/work/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import CaseColophon from '../../components/work/CaseColophon.astro';
import PermittingDiagram from '../../components/work/diagrams/PermittingDiagram.astro';
import { ORGS } from '../../data/site';

// The machine-readable restatement of this page. Every claim below is made in
// plain language somewhere in the body copy.
const work = {
  name: 'Public Health System Modernization, Florida Department of Health',
  description:
    'A county pool permitting program run on email, a spreadsheet and staff memory, rebuilt on Microsoft 365 as a single automated system of record. Michael Doerffler took over the technical build after it stalled and carried it through system architecture, structured Microsoft Forms intake, seven Power Automate flows, a SharePoint list holding fifty-one migrated historical records, role-based views separating day-to-day permit management from a stripped-back inspector view, testing, deployment, staff training and a documented handoff in July 2026.',
  about: [ORGS.doh, ORGS.troubadour],
  temporalCoverage: '2026-04/2026-07',
  keywords: [
    'Public-sector technology',
    'Process automation',
    'Microsoft 365',
    'Microsoft Forms',
    'SharePoint Lists',
    'Power Automate',
    'Workflow automation',
    'System of record',
    'Role-based views',
    'Requirements gathering',
    'Deployment and handoff',
  ],
};

const facts = [
  { label: 'CLIENT', value: 'Florida DOH · Manatee County (via Troubadour Health)' },
  { label: 'SECTOR', value: 'Government · Environmental public health' },
  { label: 'ROLE', value: 'Primary responsibility · system architecture and implementation' },
  { label: 'TIMELINE', value: 'Spring to Summer 2026' },
];
---

<Base
  title="Public Health System Modernization · Michael Doerffler"
  description="How I took over the stalled technical build of a county permitting project and delivered a working Microsoft 365 system of record for the Florida Department of Health."
  ogImage="/og/permit-modernization.png"
  breadcrumb="Public Health System Modernization"
  ogType="article"
  work={work}
>
  <CaseStudyLayout
    caseLabel="FLORIDA DOH · PERMIT MODERNIZATION"
    eyebrow="GOVERNMENT · PROCESS AUTOMATION"
    titleLine1="From a shared inbox"
    titleLine2="to a system of record."
    standfirst="A county health department's pool permitting modernization had stalled. The CEO of Troubadour Health, a public health consulting firm that puts data against complex public health problems, reached out to me directly. What began as helping accelerate it became owning the end-to-end technical build: one automated system of record, and data the department could actually manage the work with."
    nextLabel="A-LIGN"
    nextHref="/work/prospecting-automation/"
  >
    <CaseColophon items={facts} />

    <section class="case-section">
      <h2 class="sec-label" data-reveal>THE CHALLENGE</h2>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        More than fifty pool permits a year arrived by email. Details were retyped into a
        spreadsheet, status was tracked by inbox and memory, and every letter was typed by hand.
        Applications routinely arrived incomplete, and nobody knew until someone opened them.
      </p>
      <p class="situation-stmt" data-reveal>
        But the deeper problem wasn't the manual labor at all. It was that the department
        <span class="situation-key">couldn't see its own work</span>.
      </p>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        Status lived in four places at once and none of them agreed, which meant leadership could not
        answer a single basic management question:
      </p>
      <ul class="questions" data-reveal>
        <li>How long does a permit actually take?</li>
        <li>Where do they stall?</li>
        <li>Is the statutory thirty-day review clock being met?</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        Those answers weren't hard to get. They were structurally impossible, because the data didn't
        exist in any form you could ask a question of.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section case-exhibit">
      <PermittingDiagram />
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <h2 class="sec-label" data-reveal>WHAT GOT BUILT</h2>
      <p class="situation-stmt" data-reveal>One intake, one record, seven flows.</p>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        Intake is a Microsoft Form, designed against two things at once: the spreadsheet staff
        already lived in, and the DH 4159 state form. Matching both meant the new system produced
        records staff would recognize and the state would accept. The SharePoint list behind it is
        the program's first real system of record, with fifty-one historical records migrated in
        and status values rebuilt to match the milestones the manager already used rather than
        generic labels. The seven flows that move a permit between those milestones run on Power
        Automate.
      </p>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        The views split by who is looking. Day-to-day permit management sits in one, a stripped-back
        inspector view in another, so each role opens to the fields its own work actually needs.
      </p>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        A version of this system already existed in another county, built for septic permits.
        Copying it was the fast path and the wrong one. Most of the early rework came from the gap
        between what pools actually need and what septic had been built for.
      </p>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        Much of the calendar went to the parts you can't see: getting access provisioned inside a
        government tenant, and getting the consultant, the department, and government IT aligned on
        who could touch what. Once both were in place, the core system came together in a few weeks.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <h2 class="sec-label" data-reveal>RIGHT-SIZING AGAINST REALITY</h2>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        I planned for more than I built. The original design called for heavier automation and a
        much larger list.
      </p>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        I pulled back, for reasons about the client rather than the code. The team maintaining it is
        small, there is no dedicated technical staff to fix it when something breaks, and this was
        an active production project in a government tenant.
      </p>
      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        Mid-build, more conversations with the EPH manager kept sharpening the picture of what
        would actually benefit the team. We made keep-or-cut decisions against the original design,
        and most of them were cuts: fewer columns in the lists, fewer and shallower automations,
        fewer views. The wide version I had drawn got molded into the shape of their actual
        practice, something the team would be glad to work in rather than something that overwhelms
        them back into the spreadsheet.
      </p>
      <p class="closing-note" data-reveal>
        What got built is smaller than what was drawn. That was the point.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section quote-band" data-reveal>
      <div class="sec-label quote-band-label">FROM THE ENGAGEMENT LEAD</div>
      <blockquote class="client-quote">
        <p class="cq-text">
          "The technical requirements ultimately exceeded the initial scope of the engagement and
          those typically expected of an internship. Michael effectively assumed primary
          responsibility for the system architecture and implementation."
        </p>
        <footer class="cq-attr">
          Joshua T. Barnett, PhD
          <span class="cq-role">CEO, Troubadour Health</span>
        </footer>
      </blockquote>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <h2 class="sec-label" data-reveal>THE IMPACT</h2>

      <div class="findings" data-reveal>
        <div class="finding">
          <h3 class="finding-title">One centralized record</h3>
          <p class="finding-line">
            A structured electronic intake system feeding one system of record, in place of four
            disagreeing copies. Every permit timestamped, moving through defined milestones.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="finding">
          <h3 class="finding-title">Efficiency, with oversight preserved</h3>
          <p class="finding-line">
            Work that ran on staff memory now runs on its own, incomplete applications are rejected
            at intake, and staff keep every decision.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="finding">
          <h3 class="finding-title">The baseline that never existed</h3>
          <p class="finding-line">
            Handed off in July 2026, the system's first product is measurement itself: volume,
            cycle time, where permits stall, whether the thirty-day clock is met.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="finding">
          <h3 class="finding-title">A framework built to expand</h3>
          <p class="finding-line">
            Runs on Microsoft 365 licensing the department already owned, and the same architecture
            extends to food service, lodging, groupcare, and septic.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="body" data-reveal>
        It closed the way engagements should: a leadership summary, a staff guide, and a live
        walkthrough to train the team. The EPH manager, the consultant, and the IT team were
        genuinely excited to see the system come to life, and to get their time back from the
        repetitive manual work it replaces.
      </p>
      <p class="closing-note" data-reveal>
        The difference between a system that gets used and one that gets abandoned is almost
        entirely what happens in the last week.
      </p>
    </section>
  </CaseStudyLayout>
</Base>

<style>
  .case-section { margin-top: 9vh; }
  .case-exhibit { margin-top: 6vh; }

  /* Big display statement (the pivot / lead lines) */
  .situation-stmt {
    margin: 5vh 0 0;
    max-width: 1050px;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(22px, 2.4vw, 36px);
    line-height: 1.35;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .situation-key {
    color: var(--accent);
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--accent);
  }

  /* Standard body copy */
  .body {
    margin: 3.5vh 0 0;
    max-width: 900px;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: clamp(16px, 1.3vw, 18px);
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--ink-muted-2);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* The unanswerable management questions */
  .questions {
    margin: 4vh 0 0;
    max-width: 900px;
    padding: 2px 0 2px 26px;
    border-left: 2px solid rgba(46, 74, 58, 0.35);
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 13px;
  }
  .questions li {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(17px, 1.5vw, 22px);
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--ink-muted-2);
  }

  /* Impact findings: report-style hairline rows, serif finding + one line */
  .findings { margin-top: 5vh; }
  .finding {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(220px, 300px) 1fr;
    gap: 0 3vw;
    padding: 3.8vh 0;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(41, 33, 23, 0.1);
  }
  .finding:last-child { border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(41, 33, 23, 0.1); }
  .finding-title {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(18px, 1.7vw, 24px);
    line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .finding-line {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 72ch;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1.2vw, 17px);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink-muted-2);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* Closing statement */
  .closing-note {
    margin: 5vh 0 0;
    max-width: 820px;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(20px, 2vw, 30px);
    line-height: 1.35;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* Client pull-quote: green panel */
  .quote-band {
    background: rgba(46, 74, 58, 0.88);
    border-radius: 14px;
    padding: 4.5vh 3.5vw;
    max-width: 1000px;
  }
  .quote-band .quote-band-label { color: rgba(233, 225, 207, 0.75); }
  .client-quote {
    margin: 2.5vh 0 0;
    max-width: 820px;
  }
  .cq-text {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1.3vw, 18px);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--bone);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .cq-attr {
    margin-top: 2.5vh;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 4px;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: var(--bone);
  }
  .cq-role { color: rgba(233, 225, 207, 0.6); font-weight: 400; }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .case-section { margin-top: 7vh; }
    .finding { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 8px; padding: 3vh 0; }
  }
</style>
